WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.167
Account 17 has two cards with 75 euros each.

2
00:00:06.167 --> 00:00:10.500
The rule: the account's total must never go below zero.

3
00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:14.011
Two withdrawals of 100 euros start at once.

4
00:00:14.011 --> 00:00:20.249
Each reads both cards from its own snapshot, sees a total of 150, and goes ahead.

5
00:00:20.433 --> 00:00:25.400
Withdrawal A takes 100 from card 1, and B takes 100 from card 2.

6
00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:28.660
Different rows, so no lock ever conflicts.

7
00:00:28.833 --> 00:00:33.142
Under snapshot isolation both commit, and the total is minus 50.

8
00:00:33.142 --> 00:00:36.441
Run one at a time, the second would have refused.

9
00:00:36.667 --> 00:00:38.708
Run them again at SERIALIZABLE.

10
00:00:38.708 --> 00:00:44.109
Reads still never wait, but each read leaves a note on the row saying who read it.

11
00:00:44.109 --> 00:00:45.689
The notes block nothing.

12
00:00:45.833 --> 00:00:49.868
A writes a new card 1, and PostgreSQL finds B's note on it.

13
00:00:49.868 --> 00:00:56.364
B read the old value and can't see A's change, so B must come first in any one-at-a-time order.

14
00:00:56.500 --> 00:01:00.091
That's a read-write dependency, an arrow from B to A.

15
00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:04.552
Then B writes card 2, which A read: an arrow from A to B.

16
00:01:04.733 --> 00:01:07.606
Now A must come before B, and B before A.

17
00:01:07.606 --> 00:01:10.059
No one-at-a-time order can do both.

18
00:01:10.233 --> 00:01:12.471
Searching for whole cycles is slow.

19
00:01:12.471 --> 00:01:19.953
But every such cycle has a transaction with one arrow in and one out, called a pivot, so PostgreSQL watches for that.

20
00:01:20.133 --> 00:01:21.279
A commits first.

21
00:01:21.279 --> 00:01:27.869
B is a pivot, and its partner has committed, so B's commit fails with a serialization error.

22
00:01:28.067 --> 00:01:32.639
B's write is thrown away, and the total is 50, as if A ran alone.

23
00:01:32.833 --> 00:01:34.914
The application retries B.

24
00:01:34.914 --> 00:01:39.716
Its new snapshot shows 50, not enough for 100, so B refuses.

25
00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:42.462
Snapshot isolation lets both commit.

26
00:01:42.462 --> 00:01:46.447
SSI tracks who read what the other wrote and aborts one.

27
00:01:46.447 --> 00:01:47.941
Your code retries it.
